====================================================================
OBSERVATIONS
====================================================================

1. Classification across identifier renaming — the meta-R "paying rent."
   A fresh 3-cycle on unseen identifiers `{m1, m2, m3}` classifies to
   p_0 (the named 3-cycle) via canonicalization. Likewise a fresh
   2-chain on `{u, v, w}` classifies to p_2. This is the first
   concrete use of named patterns: the system recognizes that a new
   subgraph matches a previously-named structural type, without the
   caller having to encode the match logic.

2. Novel structures correctly return None.
   A 2-spoke out-star is structurally different from every named
   pattern (the named star has 3 spokes; the chain has 2 edges but
   they are sequential; the cycle has 3 edges closing back). Classify
   returns None — the system distinguishes "I haven't seen this
   structure" from "I know this."

3. Single-edge is None (not named by default γ).
   Even though ADR 0010's unfiltered naming would have called this
   p_1 (with 6 instances), the default γ policy filters single-edge.
   Querying a single edge reflects that: unmatched under the default
   policy. If a caller wants single-edges classified, they can name
   that pattern with a permissive policy — this is the principled
   choice surfacing.

4. pattern_of is symmetric with instances_of.
   Every instance identifier recovers its owning pattern. Identifiers
   that aren't instances (raw participants, the marker, bogus strings)
   all return None cleanly — there is no confusion between "is a
   participant" and "is an instance," even though both are plain
   identifiers in the single R space.

5. memberships_of reports only named-pattern participation.
   Identifiers that participate in no named pattern (c1, c5, ie1,
   ie2, t1, t2, t3, t4 — chain endpoints and the various single-edge
   participants filtered by default γ) are absent from the output.
   This lines up with the γ policy: a participant only exists in R
   because some pattern's instance recorded it; no recorded instance,
   no membership.

====================================================================
WHAT THIS ENABLES / WHAT IT DOES NOT
====================================================================

Enables:
  - Incremental classification. A caller with a subgraph in hand can
    ask "is this a known pattern?" in one method call. No need to
    re-run the pipeline or duplicate canonicalization.
  - Lookup by identifier. "Which patterns does x participate in?" is
    answerable directly, without scanning every instance.
  - Instance reconstruction. Given an instance id, recover the exact
    concrete subgraph the system recorded. Closes the loop on
    ADR 0010's "canonical form is derivable" invariant.

Does NOT enable (still open):
  - Predict missing structure. Given a partial match (e.g., two edges
    of a 3-cycle), propose the missing third edge. Requires
    completion logic — a separate, later layer.
  - Cross-graph pattern transfer. Named patterns are single-RSet.
    Carrying a pattern registry from one RSet to another is not yet
    defined.
  - Automatic attach on `add`. The query API is read-only;
    triggering naming on edge insertion is a separate hook design.

====================================================================
DECISION / NEXT
====================================================================

ADR 0013 validated. Meta-R is now actively queried, not just
recorded.

The "meta-R paying rent" transition is visible in the output: a
fresh 3-cycle with unseen identifiers is correctly mapped to p_0.
That is the first substantive use of the β layer's output.

Open directions (same shortlist as after γ, narrowed):
  - **Automatic attach on add.** Combine `classify_subgraph` with
    `run_naming_pass` so that adding new edges can extend existing
    patterns without a manual second γ call.
  - **Completion / prediction.** Given a partial subgraph, suggest
    what's missing. Harder; involves subgraph matching against
    canonical forms with variables.
  - **Cross-graph transfer.** Serialize a pattern registry and
    apply it to a different RSet.
  - **MDL scoring** and other γ refinements still open per ADR 0012.

Leaning toward automatic attach next — it is the natural
continuation of "meta-R pays rent," with the smallest surface area
increase. Completion and transfer are larger jumps.
